-## 1. Context: generative agents meet the fidelity requirement of transport planning
+## 1. Context: multimodal mobility, user-centric transport and the fidelity requirement of planning
 
-Agent-based transport models are used to test policies before they are deployed: a new tram line, a low-emission zone, a change in parking supply. For such a test to inform a decision, the simulated population must reproduce, at least in aggregate, the travel behaviour recorded by the household travel surveys that planning authorities certify and publish (Tisséo Collectivités & AUAT, 2023). Mode choice is the behaviour on which this requirement bites hardest. The split between car, public transport, walking and cycling is the quantity planners compare against survey targets, and the one their policies aim to move.
+Knowing how people move in a multimodal transport system is a prerequisite for planning traffic and for designing networks that work. The difficulty is not only to reproduce travel patterns. It is also to build a simulation framework in which individual behaviour can be studied, behavioural hypotheses can be tested, and strategies for multimodal transport and urban traffic planning can be improved. For such a framework to inform a decision, its population must moreover reproduce, at least in aggregate, the travel behaviour recorded by the household travel surveys that planning authorities certify and publish (Tisséo Collectivités & AUAT, 2023). Mode choice, the split between car, public transport, walking and cycling, is the quantity on which this requirement bites hardest: it is what planners compare against survey targets, and what their policies aim to move.
+
+Transport systems are becoming personalised and multimodal. This is part of a wider move toward mobility centred on the user, in which public transit, walking, cycling and shared services are combined into journeys that fit individual preferences (Smith et al., 1995; Horni, Nagel & Axhausen, 2016). Simulating such systems is hard, because a person's mobility choices depend on their socioeconomic profile, on the accessibility constraints they face and on what they have experienced (Grignard et al., 2018). Oberoi (2024) makes the point for Mobility-as-a-Service: personalising the service means dealing with the heterogeneity of users, their preferences, their contexts and their accessibility needs, and this remains a major obstacle to realistic transport models. A model therefore has to be flexible enough to represent heterogeneous, adaptive travel decisions, and realistic enough for those decisions to hold at the scale of a population.
+
+Current transport simulations cope poorly with this complexity, and poorly with the data they need for calibration. Detailed trajectory and itinerary datasets, which are what reveals the fine structure of multimodal travel behaviour, are very rare (Fourez et al., 2025). They exist for a few large metropolitan areas, Tokyo being one example, and are missing almost everywhere else (Feng et al., 2024). Because calibration depends on such data, existing models transfer badly to smaller or less data-rich regions. And combining several external data sources demands heavy rule engineering, which yields brittle models, hard to scale and liable to fail when conditions were not foreseen.
 
