-The appeal of these agents for transport modelling is twofold. First, they promise to represent decisions that no tabulated variable captures: a strike, a heatwave, a news item about safety in the metro. Second, they promise to do so without the calibration data that classical approaches require. Fine-grained trajectory datasets exist for a handful of metropolitan areas and are unavailable elsewhere (Feng et al., 2024), and rule-based behaviour models are costly to write and brittle outside their design envelope. A model that reasons from a persona and a set of itineraries seems to bypass both constraints.
+The appeal of these agents answers the two difficulties above. They promise to represent decisions that no tabulated variable captures: a strike, a heatwave, a news item about safety in the metro. And they promise to do so without the calibration data that classical approaches require: a model that reasons from a persona and a set of itineraries seems to bypass both the scarcity of trajectories and the brittleness of hand-written rules.
 
